course: dictionary
lesson: 7941
title: Reason: why, and how we think
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A reason tells you why something happens or why someone does something. Look for words like «because», «for this reason» or «the reason why» to spot one. «Any secret someone asks you to keep from your parents is a reason to tell them.» Here the reason is the cause: the secret is why you should tell them.
explain: Reason has other meanings too. Reason is the power to think clearly and sensibly, so a person who thinks carefully is using reason. To reason means to think something through step by step until you reach an answer. In maths and science you may be asked to «explain your reasoning», which means say why your answer is true.
explain: Reason also appears in useful phrases. «For no reason» means without any cause. «Within reason» means sensibly, not too much. To «reason with someone» means to talk to them calmly and try to persuade them. When you meet reason in a text, read the words around it and ask: is it a cause, a way of thinking, or part of a phrase?
fact: A reason is a cause or explanation: it answers the question why.
fact: Reason is also clear, sensible thinking.
fact: To reason is to think something through step by step to reach an answer.
fact: «For this reason» and «the reason why» both introduce an explanation.
fact: «Within reason» means sensibly, not too much; «reason with someone» means try to persuade them calmly.
exercise: «Any secret someone asks you to keep from your parents is a reason to tell them.» Here reason means what? | *A cause or explanation for doing something | The power to think clearly | A loud noise | A game with rules
exercise: In maths you are asked to «explain your reasoning». What are you asked to do? | Draw a picture of a dog | Guess the answer quickly | Copy the answer from a friend | *Say why your answer is true, step by step
exercise: Which phrase means sensibly, not going too far? | Reason with someone | For no reason | *Within reason | Reasoned out loud
test: Ella says, «I stayed in for no reason.» What does «for no reason» mean? | For a very good cause | Carefully, step by step | Because it was raining hard | *Without any cause or explanation
test: Sam wants to calm his angry friend down by talking gently. Which phrase fits best? | Within reason | For no reason | *Reason with someone | Reasoned it out loud
exam: «The real reasons are better: astronauts float because the Earth spins» — what does «reasons» mean here? | Rules that stop you doing things | *The explanations, the causes behind the facts | The loud noises that come from space | Games played on the Earth
